START: # sc appeal 105 2012  Page 1 of 10 IN THE SUPREME COURT OF THE DEMOCRATIC SOCIALIST REPUBLIC OF SRI LANKA In the matter of an application for leave to appeal against the judgment dated 08/11/2011 of the Civil Appeal High Court of Kurunegala in the case No. NWP/HCCA/KUR/120/2004(F) S.C. Appeal No. 105/2012 S.C.(HC) CALA Application No. 532/11 Civil Appeal, High Court of NWP Kurunegala Appeal No. NWP/HCCA/KUR/120/2004(F) DC Kurunegala Case No. 5094/L/96 01.Hitihamy Mudiyanselage Tilakaratna Banda Waraddana, Wellawa. 02.Hitihamy Mudiyanselage Punchiralage Ran Banda Waraddana, Wellawa. Plaintiffs Vs. 01.Hitihamy Mudiyanselage Subarath Menike Rangama, Wellawa. 02.Hitihamy Mudiyanselage Punchi Menike Hunupola. 03.Hithihamy Mudiyanselage Sittamma Basnayake â€œMawatteâ€, Deliwala, Rambukkana. 04.Hithihamy Mudiyanselage Pemawathi Wickremaratne No. 121, Municipal Road, Matale Page 2 of 10 05.Alkegama Alahakoon Mudiyanselage Wimala Panamaldeniya Kaluhendiwela No.119, Wadakada, Pothuhera Defendants And between 01.Hitihamy Mudiyanselage Subarath Menike Rangama, Wellawa. 02.Hitihamy Mudiyanselage Punchi Menike Hunupola. 03.Hithihamy Mudiyanselage Sittamma Basnayake â€œMawatteâ€, Deliwala, Rambukkana. 04.Hithihamy Mudiyanselage Pemawathi Wickremaratne No. 121, Municipal Road, Matale 1 st to 4 th Defendants-Appellants Vs. 01.Hitihamy Mudiyanselage Tilakaratna Banda Waraddana, Wellawa. 02.Hitihamy Mudiyanselage Punchiralage Ran Banda Waraddana, Wellawa. Plaintiff-Respondents Page 3 of 10 05.Alkegama Alahakoon Mudiyanselage Wimala Panamaldeniya Kaluhendiwela No.119, Wadakada, Pothuhera 5 th Defendant-Respondent AND NOW BETWEEN 01.Hitihamy Mudiyanselage Tilakaratna Banda Waraddana, Wellawa. 1 st Plaintiff-Respondentï¿¾Appellant 02.Hitihamy Mudiyanselage Punchiralage Ran Banda (Deceased)
END: …à¶ºà·’à¶­à·’ à¶šà·”à¶¹à·”à¶»à·™(à¶¶à¶§à·”à¶¯à¶œà·à¶½à·Šà¶½à¶¯à¶½à·Šà¶½ à¶¯à·„à·Šà¶± à·™à·à¶±à¶§ à¶šà·”à¶¹à·”à¶») à¶¶à·ƒà·Šà¶±à·à·„à·’à¶»à¶§: à¶©à¶‚à¶œà·’à¶»à·’à¶¶à¶‚à¶©à·à¶§ à·ƒà·„ à¶­à·€à¶­à·Š à¶…à¶ºà¶§ à¶…à¶ºà·’à¶­à·’à·€ Page 9 of 10 à¶­à·’à¶¶à·“ à·™à·à¶±à¶§ à¶‘à¶ à·Š.à¶‘à¶¸à·Š à¶´à¶‚à¶ à·’à¶»à·à¶½à¶§ à¶…à¶ºà·’à¶­à·’ à¶‰à¶©à¶¸à·™,à¶ºà¶± à¶¯à¶¸à¶šà·“ à¶¸à·à¶ºà·’à¶¸à·Š à·”à·… à¶´à·’à·„à·’à¶§à·’ à¶»à·–à¶© à·”à¶±à¶ºà·’ à¶´à¶»à·Šà¶ à·ƒà·Š à·€à·’à·ƒà·’à·„à¶ºà¶šà·Š (à¶…,00.à¶»à·”.03.à¶´à·à¶»à·Š.26) à·€à·’à·à·à¶½ à¶‰à¶©à¶¸à·Š à¶¯à·šâ€ Thus, both parties sought to identify the land they are claiming by reference to Plan No. 1247/à¶šà·”à¶»à·” prepared by A.B.M. Webber, which appears at page 209 of the Appeal brief marked as â€œXâ€. In these circumstances, the learned High Court judges made a grave error in law by seeking to revisit the question of the identity of the corpus. Moreover, the 1 st to 4th Defendants pleaded prescriptive title. A person who claims a prescriptive title must demonstrate possession over a specifically identified corpus; failure to do so renders such a claim untenable, as a prescription cannot operate over an uncertain or unidentified land. Accordingly, when a party claims prescriptive title to a particular corpus, that party cannot thereafter contend that the corpus has not been properly identified. The rationale is that, in order to claim prescriptive title, the land must first be clearly and specifically identified, as only then can the claimant rely on undisturbed and uninterrupted possession to establish ownership by prescription. Therefore, in the present case, the 1 st to 4th Defendants are estopped from asserting that the corpus has not been identified. For all the foregoing reasons, I answer the two questions of law in the affirmative. Accordingly, I set aside the judgment of the Civil Appeal High Court of Kurunegala dated 08.11.2011 and affirm the judgment of the learned District Judge of Kurunegala dated 20.09.2004. Page 10 of 10 The appeal is allowed with costs fixed at Rs. 75,000/=. JUDGE OF THE SUPREME COURT S. Thurairaja, PC, J. I agree. JUDGE OF THE SUPREME COURT A.H.M.D. Nawaz, J. I agree. JUDGE OF THE SUPREME COURT 
